{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1791623566299,"sessionID":"ses_edaec2704ffeWB9idQ36MdrQEO","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_00_mkyXsglP78rCbfHspv0C2863","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/workspace/docs/jobs/whom_assessment-time-tracking/assessment.md"},"output":"<path>/workspace/docs/jobs/whom_assessment-time-tracking/assessment.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # Assessment: time tracking\n2: \n3: id: whom\n4: status: open\n5: date: 2026-10-10\n6: author: Leander Muskalla (brief) / implementation assessment\n7: \n8: ## Scope\n9: \n10: This is an **assessment-only** document. No source files were changed.\n11: \n12: The brief: *\"the Time Tracking functionality is the weakest out of all of solyto's\n13: features … visually confusing and inconsistent … the data model doesn't feel\n14: intuitive (the way you add projects, categories, entries) … the way you navigate\n15: doesn't feel intuitive. It has a few good components, but it doesn't feel as easy\n16: to use and natural as the other features.\"*\n17: \n18: Every claim below is grounded in the source with `file:line` references. This\n19: repo is frontend-only, so `src/lib/types/time_tracking.ts` and\n20: `src/lib/config/apiRoutes.ts` are treated as the API contract. There are no\n21: screenshots or render reports for this job, so visual observations are derived\n22: from the templates/classes, not from rendered pixels.\n23: \n24: Files reviewed: the whole of `src/lib/components/time-tracking/`, the three\n25: `src/routes/time-tracking/**` routes, `src/lib/state/TimeTracking.svelte.ts`,\n26: `src/lib/types/time_tracking.ts`, `src/lib/config/apiRoutes.ts`, the\n27: `src/lib/components/dashboard/widgets/TimeTrackingWidget.svelte` dashboard\n28: widget, and the four i18n tables.\n29: \n30: ---\n31: \n32: ## Executive summary\n33: \n34: ### What works well\n35: \n36: - **Clean store/contract layer.** `TimeTracking` keeps categories, projects,\n37:   entries, the active timer and statistics in one runes store, loads them in\n38:   parallel, and every mutation reloads the affected collections\n39:   (`src/lib/state/TimeTracking.svelte.ts:34-152`). The REST surface is small and\n40:   legible (`src/lib/config/apiRoutes.ts:271-294`).\n41: - **Projects → categories → entries is a genuinely useful shape.** Projects own\n42:   a many-to-many set of globally-defined categories, which is a reasonable way\n43:   to slice time by both a concrete bucket and a cross-cutting tag.\n44: - **The running-timer experience is honest and correct.** A single active timer\n45:   is derived from `entries.find(e => e.stopped_at === null)`\n46:   (`src/lib/state/TimeTracking.svelte.ts:57`); `ActiveTimer` recomputes elapsed\n47:   time from `started_at` every second (`ActiveTimer.svelte:15-28`) rather than\n48:   drifting with a counter, and cleans up its interval (`ActiveTimer.svelte:31-33`).\n49: - **The project-detail page is the best-organised surface.** Header with title,\n50:   description and category chips, a clear total-time card, date-grouped entries,\n51:   and a confirmed delete (`src/routes/time-tracking/[id]/+page.svelte:102-187`).\n52: - **Reuse of shared UI where it happens.** `ProjectCreate`/`EntryCreate`/\n53:   `EntryEdit` all use `ContentModal`, `TextInput`, `Select`, `DateTimeInput`,\n54:   and the standard button components; the feature is not a bespoke island.\n55: \n56: ### What is broken or weak\n57: \n58: - **View state is invisible to the URL.** The Dashboard/Entries tabs live in\n59:   `activeTab` store state; both nav links point at the *same* href and only\n60:   mutate that state (`TimeTrackingNavigation.svelte:156-181`,\n61:   `+page.svelte:17-30`). Deep links, reloads, and back/forward all reset the view\n62:   to Dashboard. This is the single biggest structural problem and is unlike the\n63:   rest of the app (notes/todos/todos-mobile use URL-driven or open-state UI).\n64: - **Category management is hidden inside the navigation sidebar** and its two\n65:   key actions are non-discoverable: rename requires a **double-click**\n66:   (`TimeTrackingNavigation.svelte:300`) and colour editing is an inline third-party\n67:   picker (`:278-288`).\n68: - **The data model has dead and misleading fields.** `project.total_duration`\n69:   exists in the contract but is never read; durations are recomputed by hand in\n70:   three places. `has_exact_times` cannot be updated via the edit request at all.\n71:   There is an `uncategorized` fallback in two breakdown widgets for a state the\n72:   create API forbids (entries must have a `project_id`).\n73: - **Create vs. edit are duplicated and drift.** `EntryCreate` and `EntryEdit`\n74:   are ~90% the same file, and they disagree on timestamp handling (raw\n75:   `datetime-local` vs. `toISOString()`) and on whether `has_exact_times` is sent.\n76: - **The dashboard answers few real questions.** It stacks a timer, an unrelated\n77:   client-only Pomodoro, two per-project breakdowns, recent entries, and the full\n78:   statistics panel onto one page (`DashboardView.svelte:68-97`). The week\n79:   breakdown logic is duplicated verbatim in the dashboard-shell widget\n80:   (`dashboard/widgets/TimeTrackingWidget.svelte:24-52`).\n81: - **Consistency and accessibility gaps.** Hard-coded hex fallbacks (`#1dbda5`,\n82:   `#888`, `#9ca3af`), raw hand-styled `<input>`s next to shared form controls,\n83:   hover-only project controls, colour-contrast risk on category chips, icon-only\n84:   buttons without labels, and six unused i18n keys.\n85: \n86: The short version: the **domain model is sound but under-used**, the **CRUD layer\n87: is duplicated and inconsistent**, the **navigation is stateful where it should be\n88: URL-driven**, and the **dashboard mixes concerns instead of answering questions**.\n89: None of this needs a rewrite; it needs a coherent pass to make the model the\n90: single source of truth, unify create/edit, move view state into the URL, and\n91: split the dashboard's concerns.\n92: \n93: ---\n94: \n95: ## 1. Navigation & information architecture\n96: \n97: ### 1.1 Structure\n98: \n99: - `/time-tracking` → `+layout.svelte` mounts the navigation sidebar and the\n100:   project-create modal, and loads the store (`+layout.svelte:10,22-26`).\n101: - `+page.svelte` renders either `DashboardView` or `GroupedEntries` depending on\n102:   `tt.activeTab` (`+page.svelte:17-30`).\n103: - `/time-tracking/[id]` renders the project detail (`[id]/+page.svelte:30-32`),\n104:   with an effect that redirects to the list if the project disappears\n105:   (`[id]/+page.svelte:52-56`).\n106: - The sidebar (`TimeTrackingNavigation.svelte`) holds Dashboard/Entries links,\n107:   the project list, and the category manager.\n108: \n109: This is a standard \"sidebar + content\" IA and is easy to understand. The problems\n110: are in how it is *driven*.\n111: \n112: ### 1.2 View state is not in the URL (high impact)\n113: \n114: Both navigation entries resolve to the **same** route:\n115: \n116: ```svelte\n117: <!-- TimeTrackingNavigation.svelte:156-168 (Dashboard) and :169-181 (Entries) -->\n118: <a href={resolve(urls.timeTracking)} onclick={() => { tt.activeTab = 'dashboard'; handleNavigation(); }}>\n119: <a href={resolve(urls.timeTracking)} onclick={() => { tt.activeTab = 'entries';  handleNavigation(); }}>\n120: ```\n121: \n122: `activeTab` defaults to `'dashboard'` (`TimeTracking.svelte.ts:26`) and is never\n123: persisted or derived from `page.url`. Consequences:\n124: \n125: - **Reload** always lands on Dashboard, even if the user was on Entries.\n126: - **Deep-linking** to the entries view is impossible.\n127: - **Back/forward** cannot restore the view: both links push the *same* URL, and\n128:   the store is not reset, so history navigation silently does nothing (or leaves\n129:   a stale tab).\n130: - The active-tab highlight depends on `activeTab`, so it too can desync from the\n131:   URL (`TimeTrackingNavigation.svelte:159-160,172-173`).\n132: \n133: Sibling features do better: `/notes` and `/todos` represent the selected\n134: category/view through routes (`src/routes/notes/[id]`) or through explicit\n135: open/stack state with real overlays, and use the URL to restore context. Time\n136: tracking is the outlier.\n137: \n138: **Suggested direction (not code):** make the tab part of the route (e.g. a nested\n139: route or a `?tab=` search param read via `page.url.searchParams`), or remove the\n140: dual-tab model entirely and treat Entries as a route. At minimum, derive\n141: `activeTab` from the URL and write it on click.\n142: \n143: ### 1.3 Mobile drawer uses a different mechanism than every other feature\n144: \n145: `TimeTrackingNavigation` toggles the drawer by mutating inline style:\n146: \n147: ```svelte\n148: <!-- TimeTrackingNavigation.svelte:68-78 -->\n149: function toggleMobile() { if (navigation) navigation.style.display = 'block'; }\n150: function handleNavigation() { if (navigation && navigation.style.display === 'block') navigation.style.display = 'none'; }\n151: ```\n152: \n153: The container is `hidden … lg:flex` (`:142-146`), so on mobile it is revealed by\n154: the inline `display:block`; it is closed by re-setting `display='none'`.\n155: \n156: Every other feature uses an `open` `$state` flag plus `{#if open}` and a Svelte\n157: `transition:fly`/`fade`, e.g. `TodoNavigationMobile.svelte:23,63-69` and\n158: `NoteNavigationMobile.svelte:18,42-46`. The time-tracking drawer:\n159: \n160: - has no enter/exit transition (the others slide in),\n161: - can get stuck in the wrong display mode across a resize (inline `display`\n162:   outranks the `lg:flex` class),\n163: - exposes nothing to the DOM/state model that tests or focus management can use.\n164: \n165: This is a concrete instance of the brief's \"doesn't feel intuitive/consistent\".\n166: \n167: ### 1.4 Affordances hidden in the sidebar\n168: \n169: Project create/edit/delete and *all* category management live inside the\n170: navigation drawer (`TimeTrackingNavigation.svelte:184-314`). On mobile the drawer\n171: is off-screen until the hamburger is tapped; on desktop it is a 64-unit column.\n172: Consequences:\n173: \n174: - Create-project is a bare `+` icon next to the \"Projects\" heading\n175:   (`:184-198`), with no label.\n176: - Project edit/delete are **hover-only** (`opacity-0 … group-hover:opacity-100`,\n177:   `:215-217`) — unreachable by touch until the row is tapped, and effectively\n178:   invisible to new users.\n179: - Category rename is a **double-click** on the label (`:298-303`); category\n180:   delete is an always-visible trash icon that fires immediately (`:306-311`),\n181:   so the destructive action is *more* discoverable than the safe one.\n182: - The category colour picker is embedded in the list row (`:278-288`) and\n183:   commits only on click outside (`:127-132`); leaving the page without clicking\n184:   outside discards the choice (no `onDestroy` flush).\n185: \n186: ### 1.5 Where each action is reachable (matrix)\n187: \n188: | Action | Dashboard tab | Entries tab | Project detail | Nav sidebar |\n189: |---|---|---|---|---|\n190: | Start/stop timer | `TimerWidget` / `ActiveTimer` | — | — | — |\n191: | Add entry | — | `+ Add Entry` (`+page.svelte:23-26`) | `Add Entry` (`[id]/+page.svelte:139-142`) | — |\n192: | Edit entry | — (recent entries are read-only, `DashboardView.svelte:91-93`) | via `Entry` edit (`GroupedEntries.svelte:50-56`) | — (delete only, `[id]/+page.svelte:179`) | — |\n193: | Delete entry | — | `Entry` trash (`Entry.svelte:64-66`) | `Entry` trash (`[id]/+page.svelte:179`) | — |\n194: | Create project | timer/breakdown empty states only | same | — | `+` button (`:189-197`) |\n195: | Edit project | — | — | pen button (`[id]/+page.svelte:143-147`) | hover pen (`:218-228`) |\n196: | Delete project | — | — | confirmed (`[id]/+page.svelte:148-152,90-100`) | immediate (`:229-238`) |\n197: | Manage categories | — | — | — | add/rename/recolour/delete (`:244-314`) |\n198: \n199: The matrix shows the inconsistency directly: the *same* project can be deleted\n200: with confirmation on one screen and without on another, and entries are editable\n201: from exactly one of three surfaces that show them.\n202: \n203: ---\n204: \n205: ## 2. Data model & domain semantics\n206: \n207: ### 2.1 The model (the contract)\n208: \n209: ```ts\n210: // src/lib/types/time_tracking.ts\n211: interface TimeTrackingCategory { id: number; title: string; color: string | null; … }\n212: interface TimeTrackingProject  { id: string; title; description; categories: TimeTrackingCategory[];\n213:                                  total_duration: number; … }            // :9-17\n214: interface TimeTrackingEntry    { id: string; description; started_at; stopped_at: string | null;\n215:                                  duration_minutes: number; has_exact_times: boolean;\n216:                                  project: TimeTrackingProject | null; … } // :19-29\n217: ```\n218: \n219: - Categories are **global** (fetched once from `/time-tracking/categories`), and\n220:   a project links to them via `category_ids` on create/update\n221:   (`CreateTimeTrackingProjectRequest.category_ids?: number[]`, `:44`).\n222: - An entry **must** belong to a project: `CreateTimeTrackingEntryRequest` makes\n223:   `project_id: string` required (`:53-60`), and the timer's `StartTimerRequest`\n224:   too (`:70-73`). Yet the read model still types `project` as nullable (`:26`).\n225: \n226: This is a reasonable model. The issues are in how the client *uses* it.\n227: \n228: ### 2.2 `total_duration` is dead weight\n229: \n230: `TimeTrackingProject.total_duration` (`:14`) is declared but **never read**\n231: anywhere in `src/`. Instead the client recomputes totals by hand in at least\n232: three places:\n233: \n234: - `TimeTrackingNavigation.svelte:38-47` builds a `projectDurations` map by\n235:   filtering and reducing entries.\n236: - `src/routes/time-tracking/[id]/+page.svelte:44-50` reduces the same entries for\n237:   the project's total.\n238: - `DashboardView.svelte` and `TimeTrackingWidget.svelte` re-derive week/today\n239:   totals from raw entries (`DashboardView.svelte:43-65`,\n240:   `TimeTrackingWidget.svelte:41-52`).\n241: \n242: So there are **two competing sources of truth** for the same number (API field\n243: vs. client reduction), and the client chose the more expensive one. The API also\n244: exposes a statistics endpoint (`statistics: '/time-tracking/entries/statistics'`,\n245: `apiRoutes.ts:292`) that the dashboard ignores for its own breakdowns.\n246: \n247: **Suggested direction:** pick one authority. Either consume `total_duration` and\n248: the statistics endpoint, or formally deprecate the field. Do not keep both.\n249: \n250: ### 2.3 The `uncategorized` state cannot exist\n251: \n252: Two breakdown widgets carry a fallback for entries with no project:\n253: \n254: ```ts\n255: const key   = project?.title ?? ts.get.timeTracking.uncategorized;\n256: const color = project?.categories?.[0]?.color ?? '#9ca3af';\n257: ```\n258: \n259: `DashboardView.svelte:32-33`, `dashboard/widgets/TimeTrackingWidget.svelte:31`.\n260: But the create contract requires `project_id`, and the timer requires a project,\n261: so a project-less entry should be impossible. Either the fallback is dead code\n262: (remove it and simplify) or there is a real server-returned case the client\n263: should handle deliberately (a proper \"unassigned\" bucket with UX). Right now it\n264: is quietly carrying a hard-coded grey.\n265: \n266: ### 2.4 `has_exact_times` cannot be edited\n267: \n268: The flag is:\n269: - **sent** on create (`EntryCreate.svelte:63,89`, `CreateTimeTrackingEntryRequest.has_exact_times`),\n270: - **read** for display (`Entry.svelte:49-54`),\n271: - but **absent** from `UpdateTimeTrackingEntryRequest` (`types/time_tracking.ts:62-68`).\n272: \n273: `EntryEdit` therefore never sends it (`EntryEdit.svelte:68-77`). Editing an\n274: entry to add exact start/stop times updates `started_at`/`stopped_at` but leaves\n275: `has_exact_times` at its old value, so the UI may keep showing (or hiding) the\n276: time range incorrectly after an edit. The domain concept (\"do we have real clock\n277: times or just a duration?\") leaks into the UI without a way to keep it correct.\n278: \n279: ### 2.5 Backdating is misleading (`buildTimestamps`)\n280: \n281: ```ts\n282: // EntryCreate.svelte:65-76\n283: const now  = new Date();\n284: const end  = new Date(`${date}T${now.getHours()}:${now.getMinutes()}:00`);\n285: const start = new Date(end.getTime() - durationMinutes * 60000);\n286: return { started_at: start.toISOString(), stopped_at: end.toISOString() };\n287: ```\n288: \n289: The **calendar day** comes from the user's `date`, but the **time of day** is\n290: always *the moment the entry is created*. So:\n291: \n292: - A duration-only entry added for a past day is stamped with today's wall-clock\n293:   time on that past day — an arbitrary-looking start/stop that the user never\n294:   chose and cannot see (the entry renders as a bare duration unless\n295:   `has_exact_times` is set, `Entry.svelte:49-54`).\n296: - If the duration exceeds the current time-of-day, `start` rolls into the\n297:   **previous calendar day**, so an entry \"for 2026-10-01\" can silently begin on\n298:   2026-09-30. The date grouping (`[id]/+page.svelte:58-70`,\n299:   `GroupedEntries.svelte:18-30`) then places it under the wrong day.\n300: - The visible `date` field stays on screen even when the exact-times section is\n301:   expanded (`EntryCreate.svelte:130-139` vs. `:149-164`), where it is ignored —\n302:   the user can change it with no effect.\n303: \n304: **Suggested direction:** for duration-only entries, anchor at a defined\n305: time (e.g. local midnight, or noon, or the user's chosen time) and compute the\n306: day from that anchor, not from \"now\". Hide the date field when exact times are in\n307: use, or make the two inputs one coherent control.\n308: \n309: ### 2.6 Category colour is overloaded as the project colour\n310: \n311: A project has **many** categories, each with a colour, but the breakdowns colour\n312: the project by its **first** category:\n313: \n314: ```ts\n315: const color = project?.categories?.[0]?.color ?? '#9ca3af';\n316: // DashboardView.svelte:33, TimeTrackingWidget.svelte:31\n317: ```\n318: \n319: If a project's first category changes, the project's chart colour changes. If a\n320: project has several categories, the other colours vanish. The statistics endpoint\n321: does return a per-project `color` (`TimeTrackingStatisticsEntry.color`,\n322: `types/time_tracking.ts:75-80`) which `StatisticsView:47` uses — so the donut and\n323: the breakdowns can disagree about a project's colour. A project needs its own\n324: identity/colour, separate from its category tags.\n325: \n326: ### 2.7 What the model makes easy vs. awkward\n327: \n328: **Easy / natural:** start a timer against a project in one click; add a manual\n329: duration; tag projects with reusable categories; view date-grouped history.\n330: \n331: **Awkward:** there is no way to record \"I worked on X\" without first creating a\n332: project; categories are mandatory to get a coloured chart (else grey); the\n333: project's own total is recomputed rather than trusted; and \"when did this happen\"\n334: cannot be expressed honestly without discovering the hidden \"started at / stopped\n335: at\" disclosure.\n336: \n337: ---\n338: \n339: ## 3. Create / edit / manage flows (CRUD friction)\n340: \n341: ### 3.1 `ProjectCreate` doubles as edit (acceptable, with rough edges)\n342: \n343: One modal handles create and edit via `projectId`\n344: (`ProjectCreate.svelte:13-18`), titles itself from `existingProject`\n345: (`:69`), and pre-fills fields. Good.\n346: \n347: Rough edges:\n348: - Empty title silently no-ops: `if (!title.trim()) return;` (`:44-45`) — no\n349:   message, the Save button appears to do nothing.\n350: - Category selection is a `+` that opens a dropdown of remaining categories\n351:   (`:106-133`); removing a category is an unlabelled `×` (`:98-103`). Both use\n352:   the generic dashed-border style rather than a shared control.\n353: - Colours are hard-coded in two places: chips fall back to `#888` (`:95`) and\n354:   the dropdown dots render only `{#if cat.color}` (`:121-126`), so a category\n355:   without a colour shows no dot at all.\n356: \n357: ### 3.2 `EntryCreate` vs. `EntryEdit`: ~90% duplicate, and they disagree\n358: \n359: The two files share the same structure: project `Select`, description\n360: `TextInput`, duration amount + unit, an exact-times disclosure with two\n361: `DateTimeInput`s, and a save button. But:\n362: \n363: | Aspect | `EntryCreate` | `EntryEdit` |\n364: |---|---|---|\n365: | Date field | present (`:130-139`) | absent |\n366: | Default project | first project (`:33`) | entry's project, else first (`:31`) |\n367: | Exact-times initial state | collapsed (`:30`) | collapsed **even if `entry.has_exact_times`** (`:32`) |\n368: | Sends `has_exact_times` | yes (`:89`) | no (field absent from update type) |\n369: | Exact timestamps sent as | raw `datetime-local` value (`:67`) | `new Date(...).toISOString()` (`:75-76`) |\n370: | `started_at` init | empty | `entry.started_at.slice(0,16)` (`:33`) |\n371: \n372: Two concrete bugs fall out of this:\n373: \n374: 1. **Timezone handling differs.** `EntryCreate` sends the raw `datetime-local`\n375:    string (`\"2026-10-01T09:00\"`, no zone), while `EntryEdit` converts via\n376:    `toISOString()` (UTC). The same times entered in create and edit produce\n377:    different instants. Worse, `EntryEdit` initialises the control from\n378:    `entry.started_at.slice(0,16)` — an ISO/UTC string — and feeds it to a\n379:    `datetime-local` input, which interprets it as *local* time, so an existing\n380:    entry's times shift by the UTC offset the moment the disclosure is opened.\n381: 2. **Editing the date is impossible** without expanding the exact-times\n382:    disclosure (there is no date field), even though the entry has a date.\n383: \n384: `EntryCreate` also `$derived`s `hasExactTimes = showExactTimes && !!(startedAt && stoppedAt)`\n385: (`:63`) but only sends it on create; and the disclosure can be open with only one\n386: timestamp filled, in which case `hasExactTimes` is false and `buildTimestamps`\n387: falls back to \"now\" (`:65-76`) while the half-filled inputs remain on screen.\n388: \n389: ### 3.3 Silent validation\n390: \n391: Both entry forms share the same silent guard:\n392: \n393: ```ts\n394: if (!projectId || durationMinutes <= 0) return;   // EntryCreate.svelte:79; EntryEdit.svelte:64\n395: ```\n396: \n397: No error, no disabled-button feedback. A user who types a duration but hasn't\n398: picked a project (or types 0) clicks Save and nothing happens. `TextButton` is\n399: always enabled (`EntryCreate.svelte:166`). This is a recurring source of\n400: \"the app feels unresponsive\".\n401: \n402: ### 3.4 Default-to-first-project\n403: \n404: Three components silently default the project to `tt.projects[0]`:\n405: \n406: - `TimerWidget.svelte:14`\n407: - `EntryCreate.svelte:33`\n408: - `EntryEdit.svelte:31` (fallback only)\n409: \n410: The project order is `tt.projects` as returned by the API; there is no \"most\n411: recent\" or alphabetic guarantee. So a manual entry or a timer can be silently\n412: booked against whichever project happens to be first — a data-integrity risk\n413: that is easy to miss because the chosen project is shown but not obvious.\n414: \n415: ### 3.5 Delete inconsistency\n416: \n417: | Thing | Confirmation | Location |\n418: |---|---|---|\n419: | Project (detail page) | `ConfirmationModal` (`[id]/+page.svelte:90-100`) | explicit |\n420: | Project (sidebar) | **none** (`TimeTrackingNavigation.svelte:229-238`) | immediate |\n421: | Category | **none** (`:306-311`) | immediate |\n422: | Entry | **none** (`Entry.svelte:64-66`) | immediate |\n423: \n424: The project case is the starkest: deleting from the detail page asks and removes\n425: associated entries (`deletion_description`, `i18n/en.ts:1109-1110`), but the same\n426: destructive call from the sidebar is one click with no prompt and no undo. Entry\n427: and category deletes have no undo anywhere.\n428: \n429: ### 3.6 Editing availability\n430: \n431: - Entries are editable **only** from the Entries tab, via `GroupedEntries` →\n432:   `Entry showEdit` (`GroupedEntries.svelte:50-56`).\n433: - The dashboard's \"Recent Entries\" are read-only (`DashboardView.svelte:91-93`).\n434: - The project detail page shows entries with delete but **not** edit\n435:   (`[id]/+page.svelte:179`, `Entry showDelete` only).\n436: \n437: So a user who spots a mistake while looking at a project's history must navigate\n438: to the Entries tab to fix it — the surface that shows the entry doesn't let you\n439: edit it.\n440: \n441: ---\n442: \n443: ## 4. Timer, dashboard & statistics\n444: \n445: ### 4.1 Timer\n446: \n447: - `TimerWidget` shows `ActiveTimer` when `tt.activeTimer` is set, otherwise a\n448:   project `Select`, a description input, and a Start button\n449:   (`TimerWidget.svelte:34-56`). The mutual exclusivity is correct and readable.\n450: - `ActiveTimer` derives elapsed from `started_at` each tick\n451:   (`ActiveTimer.svelte:15-28`) and destroys its interval (`:31-33`) — sound.\n452: - The start form resets `timerDescription` after a start (`TimerWidget.svelte:29`),\n453:   but does **not** reset/re-select the project, which is fine for repeat starts.\n454: \n455: Honest state: yes — one timer, derived from entries, not a separate flag. The one\n456: gap is that `startTimer` (`TimeTracking.svelte.ts:132-136`) has no client guard\n457: against starting a second timer; it relies on the UI not offering Start when a\n458: timer is active. Acceptable, but a defensive check would be cheap.\n459: \n460: ### 4.2 Pomodoro is a fourth, unintegrated timer\n461: \n462: `PomodoroWidget` is a fully client-side interval timer (25/5/15 min, beep,\n463: session dots: `PomodoroWidget.svelte:13-16,61-76,100-121`). It:\n464: \n465: - creates **no** time entry and links to **no** project (`:1-126` — it only\n466:   imports `getTranslation`),\n467: - is not persisted (reload loses it; `onDestroy` just clears the interval,\n468:   `:123-125`),\n469: - duplicates the concept of \"a timer\" beside the real one, with a *different*\n470:   visual language (teal/amber, own buttons) than `ActiveTimer` (primary-coloured).\n471: \n472: For a feature whose purpose is \"track time spent on projects\", a prominent timer\n473: that records nothing is actively confusing — it competes with the real timer in\n474: the same grid (`DashboardView.svelte:69-75`). Either wire it to create/link an\n475: entry (and share the timer UI), or demote it out of the time-tracking dashboard.\n476: \n477: ### 4.3 Dashboard composition mixes concerns\n478: \n479: `DashboardView` is a single column that stacks (`DashboardView.svelte:68-97`):\n480: \n481: 1. a 4-up grid of Timer, Pomodoro, Today-breakdown, This-Week-breakdown,\n482: 2. recent entries (read-only),\n483: 3. the full `StatisticsView`.\n484: \n485: So the \"Dashboard\" contains a full analytics panel, and the standalone stats\n486: surface doesn't exist as its own tab. `StatisticsView` also fires its own API\n487: call on mount (`StatisticsView.svelte:70-72`), so merely opening the dashboard\n488: triggers a statistics request.\n489: \n490: The layout also has a class bug: the breakdown widgets declare\n491: `lg:col-span-1` (`TimeBreakdownWidget.svelte:18`) in a grid that is\n492: `md:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-4` (`DashboardView.svelte:69`); the Timer and\n493: Pomodoro wrappers also say `lg:col-span-1` (`:70,73`). Everything claims one\n494: column, which happens to work for exactly four items but is fragile if the set\n495: changes.\n496: \n497: ### 4.4 Breakdowns duplicate logic and colour arbitrarily\n498: \n499: `DashboardView.groupByProject` (`:26-41`) and\n500: `TimeTrackingWidget.groupByProject` (`:24-39`) are the same function, including\n501: the `#9ca3af` fallback and the first-category colour rule. `TimeBreakdownWidget`\n502: just renders whatever list it is given (`TimeBreakdownWidget.svelte:29-51`).\n503: Grouping happens by **project title**, so two projects with the same title merge.\n504: \n505: ### 4.5 Statistics\n506: \n507: - Embedded, not a tab (see 4.3).\n508: - Date range uses two raw `<input type=\"date\">`s with bespoke classes\n509:   (`StatisticsView.svelte:89-103`) rather than a shared date control; defaults are\n510:   computed from `toISOString()` (`:15-19`), i.e. UTC, so users east/west of UTC\n511:   can get a one-day-off default range.\n512: - The **category filter filters *projects*** by whether any of their categories\n513:   match (`:30-37`) and then recomputes both the donut and the category bar chart\n514:   from the filtered set (`:43-63`). Its label says \"Filter by Category\" but the\n515:   effect is \"restrict to projects tagged with these categories\" — defensible, but\n516:   it silently drops projects that have the category plus others' time.\n517: - The `DoughnutChart` uses the server's per-project `color` (`:47`) while the\n518:   breakdown widgets use first-category colour (see 2.6), so colours are not\n519:   consistent across the page.\n520: - Category bar values are converted to hours with one decimal\n521:   (`:67`, `Math.round((c.minutes / 60) * 10) / 10`) while everything else is\n522:   `h m` text — a units inconsistency inside one view.\n523: - `loadStatistics` interpolates `from`/`to` directly into a query string\n524:   (`TimeTracking.svelte.ts:147-152`) without encoding; harmless for date-only\n525:   values but worth noting.\n526: \n527: ### 4.6 Dashboard shell widget\n528: \n529: `dashboard/widgets/TimeTrackingWidget.svelte` is a *separate* week-breakdown\n530: widget used on the home dashboard (`QuickGlance.svelte:14`). It re-implements the\n531: same grouping, colour fallback and totals as `DashboardView` (`:24-52`) and calls\n532: `tt.load()` itself (`:54-59`), so the store can be loaded twice. This is a\n533: textbook DRY violation and a maintenance hazard: fixing a rule in one place\n534: misses the other.\n535: \n536: ---\n537: \n538: ## 5. Visual & interaction consistency, accessibility, i18n\n539: \n540: ### 5.1 Shared controls vs. hand-rolled inputs\n541: \n542: The feature is inconsistent about using its own design system:\n543: \n544: | Control | Uses shared component? | Evidence |\n545: |---|---|---|\n546: | Project title/description | ✅ `TextInput` | `ProjectCreate.svelte:79,85` |\n547: | Entry description | ✅ `TextInput` | `EntryCreate.svelte:112`, `EntryEdit.svelte:98` |\n548: | Project selector | ✅ `Select` | `EntryCreate.svelte:106`, `TimerWidget.svelte:42` |\n549: | Duration amount | ❌ raw `<input type=\"number\">` | `EntryCreate.svelte:119-126`, `EntryEdit.svelte:105-111` |\n550: | Date | ❌ raw `<input type=\"date\">` | `EntryCreate.svelte:134-138`, `StatisticsView.svelte:89-103` |\n551: | Timer description | ❌ raw `<input type=\"text\">` | `TimerWidget.svelte:43-48` |\n552: | Category inline rename | ❌ raw `<input type=\"text\">` | `TimeTrackingNavigation.svelte:290-296` |\n553: \n554: The raw inputs copy the shared classes by hand, which is why they have already\n555: drifted: `EntryCreate.svelte:125` uses `focus:ring-d-lightblue` and\n556: `dark:focus:ring-c-primary` — identical to `TextInput.svelte:30`, so the drift is\n557: cosmetic today, but nothing keeps them in sync. `TimerWidget`'s input uses the\n558: same recipe (`:47`). There is no shared `NumberInput`/`DateInput`, so adding one\n559: (or reusing `TextInput` with a `type`) would remove the duplication.\n560: \n561: ### 5.2 Hard-coded colours\n562: \n563: Colour literals are scattered and fall back differently per component:\n564: \n565: - `#1dbda5` (brand teal) is the category fallback in the nav\n566:   (`TimeTrackingNavigation.svelte:61,84,130`) and in the statistics filter\n567:   (`StatisticsView.svelte:134,139`).\n568: - `#888` is the fallback in `ProjectCreate.svelte:95` and on the detail page\n569:   category chips (`[id]/+page.svelte:129`).\n570: - `#9ca3af` is the breakdown fallback (`DashboardView.svelte:33`,\n571:   `TimeTrackingWidget.svelte:31`).\n572: \n573: Three different \"unknown colour\" greys/teals for the same concept. The codebase\n574: already exposes theming via CSS variables (`--color-c-primary`, etc.), so these\n575: should be variables, not literals.\n576: \n577: ### 5.3 Hover-only and touch-hostile affordances\n578: \n579: - Project edit/delete hidden until hover (`TimeTrackingNavigation.svelte:216`).\n580:   On touch there is no hover, so these only appear after a tap that also\n581:   navigates. This is the clearest \"not natural on mobile\" finding.\n582: - Category rename requires double-click (`:300`) — not discoverable, not\n583:   available via keyboard, and awkward on touch (double-tap also zooms).\n584: - `InlineEditButton`/`InlineDeleteButton` reveal their actual clickable button\n585:   only on `group-hover` (`InlineEditButton.svelte:13`,\n586:   `InlineDeleteButton.svelte:12-13`); they render an inert icon underneath, so\n587:   the visible icon is not the button. That is both an a11y and a hit-target\n588:   problem where they're used (entry rows, project header).\n589: \n590: ### 5.4 Accessibility\n591: \n592: - **Double-click to rename a category** (`TimeTrackingNavigation.svelte:300`) is\n593:   a keyboard and screen-reader dead end; nothing signals the action.\n594: - **Icon-only buttons without accessible names**: project add (`:189-197`),\n595:   project edit/delete (`:218-238`), category add (`:249-258`), category delete\n596:   (`:306-311`), and the `Inline*` buttons carry only SVG icons — no `aria-label`\n597:   or visible text.\n598: - **Colour contrast**: category chips render `text-xs text-white` on whatever\n599:   `cat.color` is (`[id]/+page.svelte:127-131`, `ProjectCreate.svelte:93-96`).\n600:   A light category colour gives white-on-light text with no contrast guard; the\n601:   statistics filter pills have the same issue (`StatisticsView.svelte:129-142`).\n602: - **Form labelling**: the entry/project fields use plain `<span>` labels, not\n603:   `<label for>`; none of the inputs are programmatically associated\n604:   (`EntryCreate.svelte:102-139`, `ProjectCreate.svelte:75-86`).\n605: - The mobile drawer's `display` toggling (1.3) gives no focus trap/restore, so\n606:   keyboard focus can move behind the open drawer.\n607: \n608: ### 5.5 i18n\n609: \n610: All four locales define the same key set and are in sync (verified for\n611: `timeTracking`: identical keys across `en.ts`, `de.ts`, `es.ts`, `fr.ts`) — good.\n612: \n613: However six `timeTracking` keys are **defined but never referenced** anywhere in\n614: `src/` (excluding the i18n tables):\n615: \n616: | Key | `en.ts` line | Notes |\n617: |---|---|---|\n618: | `add_category` | 1078 | UI uses an icon-only `+` instead |\n619: | `edit_category` | 1079 | rename is double-click, no label |\n620: | `color` | 1082 | colour picker is unlabelled |\n621: | `duration_minutes` | 1086 | duration uses a number + unit select |\n622: | `category` | 1088 | singular; only `categories` is used |\n623: | `this_month` | 1107 | dashboard shows Today/This Week only |\n624: \n625: Additionally, `dev.features.timeTracking` tips (`timer_tip`, `projects_tip`,\n626: `pomodoro_tip`, `en.ts:1062-1064`) are not consumed by any component: the\n627: welcome tour reads tips from `welcome_tour.steps`, not `dev.features`\n628: (`WelcomeTour.svelte:73-78`), and `OnboardingModalStepFeatures` uses\n629: `welcome_tour` keys (`OnboardingModalStepFeatures.svelte:22-35`). So the\n630: Pomodoro tip in particular documents a feature users are never told about — and\n631: the whole `dev.features.timeTracking` block is orphaned. These are dead strings\n632: that still cost translation effort; the unused ones should be removed (or wired\n633: up).\n634: \n635: ### 5.6 Heading/spacing conventions\n636: \n637: `DashboardView` uses `Heading` for \"Recent Entries\" (`:90`) but the statistics\n638: heading is inside `StatisticsView` (`:83`); section titles elsewhere are bare\n639: `<span>`s (`TimeBreakdownWidget.svelte:19`, `Projects`/`Categories` in the nav\n640: `:186,246`). Heading levels are therefore inconsistent across one page. Spacing\n641: is also ad-hoc (`gap-8` outer, `gap-4` grid, `gap-6`/`gap-3` sections mixed), so\n642: the vertical rhythm varies between the dashboard and the project detail page.\n643: \n644: ---\n645: \n646: ## 6. Prioritised follow-up recommendations\n647: \n648: Ranked by impact/effort. Each is a candidate for a separate implementation job;\n649: none is in scope here.\n650: \n651: | # | Recommendation | Impact | Effort | Files |\n652: |---|---|---|---|---|\n653: | 1 | **Make view state URL-driven.** Move Dashboard/Entries (and ideally the project selection) into the route or a `?tab=` param; derive `activeTab` from `page.url` so reload/deep-link/back work. | High | M | `TimeTrackingNavigation.svelte`, `+page.svelte`, `TimeTracking.svelte.ts`, `urls.ts` |\n654: | 2 | **Unify `EntryCreate`/`EntryEdit` into one component** that takes an optional `entry`; fix the timestamp/timezone mismatch (always send ISO) and add a real date control. | High | M | `EntryCreate.svelte`, `EntryEdit.svelte`, `GroupedEntries.svelte`, `+page.svelte`, `[id]/+page.svelte` |\n655: | 3 | **Fix backdating semantics** in `buildTimestamps` (defined anchor, correct day, hide the date field when exact times are used). | High | S | `EntryCreate.svelte` |\n656: | 4 | **Single source of truth for durations/colours**: consume `project.total_duration`/statistics, give projects their own colour, delete the duplicated `groupByProject` and the `#9ca3af`/`#888`/`#1dbda5` literals. | High | M | `TimeTracking.svelte.ts`, `DashboardView.svelte`, `TimeTrackingWidget.svelte`, `TimeBreakdownWidget.svelte`, `StatisticsView.svelte`, `types/time_tracking.ts` |\n657: | 5 | **Give Pomodoro a purpose or a home**: either link it to an entry/timer with shared UI, or move it out of the time-tracking dashboard. | Medium | M | `PomodoroWidget.svelte`, `DashboardView.svelte` |\n658: | 6 | **Confirm-or-undo for destructive actions.** Route all project deletes through `ConfirmationModal`; add undo for entry/category deletes. | Medium | S | `TimeTrackingNavigation.svelte`, `Entry.svelte` |\n659: | 7 | **Surface entry editing where entries are shown** (dashboard recent entries, project detail). | Medium | S | `DashboardView.svelte`, `[id]/+page.svelte`, `Entry.svelte` |\n660: | 8 | **Replace the sidebar's `display` toggle with the app's `open`-state + transition drawer pattern** used by todos/notes. | Medium | S | `TimeTrackingNavigation.svelte` |\n661: | 9 | **Make category management discoverable and accessible**: replace double-click-to-rename with an explicit affordance, add `aria-label`s to icon buttons, guard category-chip contrast, avoid hover-only project controls. | Medium | M | `TimeTrackingNavigation.svelte`, `ProjectCreate.svelte`, `Entry.svelte`, `[id]/+page.svelte`, `InlineEdit/DeleteButton.svelte` |\n662: | 10 | **Add explicit form validation feedback** instead of silent `return` on invalid save (disable Save or show a message). | Medium | S | `EntryCreate.svelte`, `EntryEdit.svelte`, `ProjectCreate.svelte` |\n663: | 11 | **Reuse shared form controls** for number/date/timer inputs (or add a `NumberInput`/`DateInput`), removing hand-copied classes. | Low | S | `EntryCreate.svelte`, `EntryEdit.svelte`, `TimerWidget.svelte`, `StatisticsView.svelte` |\n664: | 12 | **Handle or delete the `uncategorized` path** and the un-editable `has_exact_times` flag so the UI matches the contract. | Low | S | `DashboardView.svelte`, `TimeTrackingWidget.svelte`, `types/time_tracking.ts`, `EntryEdit.svelte` |\n665: | 13 | **Remove (or wire up) dead i18n keys** `add_category`, `edit_category`, `color`, `duration_minutes`, `category`, `this_month`, and the orphaned `dev.features.timeTracking` tips, across all four locales. | Low | S | `i18n/{en,de,es,fr}.ts` |\n666: | 14 | **Fix UTC-based default dates** (`new Date().toISOString()`) in entry and statistics defaults to local time. | Low | S | `EntryCreate.svelte`, `StatisticsView.svelte` |\n667: | 15 | **Normalise heading/spacing** in the dashboard and detail page via the shared `Heading` component. | Low | S | `DashboardView.svelte`, `StatisticsView.svelte`, `TimeBreakdownWidget.svelte` |\n668: \n669: Suggested sequencing: 1–4 together form the \"make the foundations coherent\" pass\n670: and unlock most of the rest; 5–10 are the \"natural-feeling interactions\" pass;\n671: 11–15 are polish.\n672: \n673: ---\n674: \n675: ## 7. Conclusion\n676: \n677: Time Tracking is not a bad feature with broken plumbing — it is a good domain\n678: model wrapped in a UI that hides its model, duplicates it, and drives view state\n679: outside the URL. The clearest wins are structural and cheap to state: put the\n680: view in the URL, unify create/edit, trust the API's own totals and colours, make\n681: category management visible, and stop the Pomodoro timer from competing with the\n682: real one. The store/contract layer and the running-timer widget are solid\n683: foundations to build on.\n684: \n685: **Scope note:** per the brief, this is an assessment only — **no source files\n686: were changed**. All recommendations are candidates for follow-up implementation\n687: jobs.\n\n(End of file - total 687 lines)\n</content>\n\n<system-reminder>\nInstructions from: /workspace/docs/AGENTS.md\n# solyto frontend\n\n<!--\nThis is your project context, loaded by the agent at the start of every session.\nmanigot is vendor-agnostic: it runs Claude Code or OpenCode against the same\nproject (`mg --profile claude-pro` vs `mg --profile zai`/`--profile\nopencode-go`), and this one file serves both — manigot mounts it read-only\nwherever the selected tool looks for it\n(/workspace/AGENTS.md for OpenCode, /workspace/.claude/CLAUDE.md for Claude\nCode). Those mount paths are read-only: to change this context, edit this\nfile (docs/AGENTS.md), never the mount paths.\nThe same global agents are available under @name either way, and custom\nproject agents in docs/agents/ work under both tools — write them in the\nbuilt-in format (name:, description:, tools: Read, Grep, ...), no per-tool\nformat needed. To make a custom agent read-only under OpenCode, add a\n`permission:` frontmatter block (the built-in format manigot's conversion\npasses through to OpenCode's schema — see the manigot README's agent section);\nthe read-only built-in agents' blocks deny the destructive git commands\n(worktree management, branch -d/-D, reset, checkout, push, ...).\nCustom agents that must commit (like the built-in developer/reviewer/quality)\ndeclare `commit: true` in their frontmatter; agents that never commit declare\n`commit: false` and get a read-only git mount. The default — no agent named,\nfile missing, or marker absent/unknown — is a writable git mount, so a\ncommitting agent is never broken by a missing marker.\nAgent sessions also restrict git to reading history and making commits (the\nsession git shim): worktree management, branch deletes, resets, checkouts,\npushes, and the other destructive subcommands are refused.\nKeep this file tool-neutral — write it for \"the agent\", not for one vendor.\n-->\n\nThe client for [solyto.app](https://solyto.app): a free, private, all-in-one personal management app — todos, notes, calendar, contacts, media libraries, check-in, finances, feeds, time tracking, clipboard, and dev-requests in one place. This repo is the frontend only: a SvelteKit 2 / Svelte 5 single-page app that runs on the web, as an installable PWA, or as a native desktop/mobile shell. There is no backend or database here — every piece of data comes from the solyto REST API, which lives in a separate repository.\n\n## Stack\n- Backend: none in this repo — pure client-side SPA. Consumes the solyto REST API at `/api/v1` (`api.solyto.app` in prod, `http://localhost:8000` in dev; configurable per hostname in `src/lib/config/platform.ts`).\n- Frontend: SvelteKit 2, Svelte 5 (runes, `$state`), TypeScript (strict), Vite 7, Tailwind CSS v4 via `@tailwindcss/vite`, adapter-node (outputs to `build/`).\n- Database: none — persistence is owned by the backend. The client only stores auth tokens, theme, and preferences in LocalStorage (`src/lib/services/LocalStorageService.ts`).\n- Key packages: `@tiptap/*` (notes rich-text editor), `echarts` (statistics charts), `@simplewebauthn/browser` (passkey login), `marked` + `dompurify` (markdown), `@lucide/svelte` (icons), `svelte-dnd-action` / `svelte-gestures` (drag & drop, swipe), `canvas-confetti`, `@fontsource/*`, `@vvo/tzdb`.\n\n## Architecture\n- `src/routes/` — SvelteKit file-based routes, all client-rendered (no `+page.server.ts`/`+server.ts` anywhere). The root `+layout.svelte` bootstraps every state store via `setContext`, guards unauthenticated access (redirect to `/auth/login` or `/setup`), and renders the navbar/footer shells. Feature routes: `/todos`, `/notes`, `/calendar`, `/contacts`, `/check-in`, `/finances`, `/feeds`, `/time-tracking`, `/clipboard`, `/dev-requests`, `/libraries/{books,movies,music,games,links,quotes,recipes,plants}`, plus `/settings`, `/admin`, `/profile`, `/auth/*`, `/setup`, `/share`.\n- `src/lib/state/` — one Svelte 5 runes class per domain (`Auth`, `Todos`, `Notes`, `Calendars`, `BookLibrary`, ...). Instances are created in `+layout.svelte` and shared through Svelte context (`setContext`/`getContext`); each store fetches via `ApiService` and holds the result in reactive `$state`.\n- `src/lib/services/` — `ApiService` (fetch wrapper over the REST API: Bearer auth, JSON, `%s`/`%d` URL placeholders, FormData uploads) and pure domain services (`TodoFilterService`, `TodoSortingService`, `TodoGroupingService`, `TodoRelevanceService`, `LibraryFilterService`, `WealthStatisticsService`, ...) that are unit-tested.\n- `src/lib/config/` — `apiRoutes.ts` (all endpoints, composed from `getApiUrl()` + `/api/v1`), `platform.ts` (web vs. desktop vs. mobile via `PUBLIC_DESKTOP`/`PUBLIC_MOBILE`, API URL resolution), `themes.ts`, `navigation.ts`, `urls.ts`, `features.ts`.\n- `src/lib/components/` — feature components plus shared `ui/` (buttons, modals, navbar), `forms/` (inputs, pickers, toggles), and `charts/` (echarts wrappers). Several features keep separate desktop/mobile view variants (e.g. `calendars/views/{day,week,month,list}`, `todos/views/`).\n- `src/lib/types/` — TypeScript interfaces mirroring the API data model, one file per domain (`todo.ts`, `note.ts`, `library_*.ts`, `calendar.ts`, ...).\n- `src/lib/i18n/` — translation tables `en.ts`, `de.ts`, `es.ts`, `fr.ts`, typed via `types/translation.ts`; every UI string must exist in all four.\n- `src/lib/helpers/` — pure utilities (DateHelper, NumberHelper, FormatHelper, ColorHelper, MusicTheory, ...).\n- `src/service-worker.ts` — PWA service worker (app-shell precache, stale-while-revalidate, push notifications); `static/site.webmanifest` is the PWA manifest, `static/themes/*` are runtime-loaded theme CSS.\n- `tests/` — Vitest unit tests for pure helpers/services only (no component or integration tests); run in node env with `TZ=UTC` and `$lib` aliased to `src/lib`.\n- Data flow: component → state store → `ApiService` → REST API. There is no server-side rendering of app data.\n\n## Commands\n- `npm run dev` — start the Vite dev server on port 5173 (expects the API at `http://localhost:8000`)\n- `npm run build` — production build via `vite build` (adapter-node, output in `build/`)\n- `npm run preview` — preview the production build locally\n- `npm run check` — type-check with `svelte-check` (a11y warnings selectively ignored)\n- `npm run lint` — `prettier --check .` + `eslint .`\n- `npm run format` — auto-format with `prettier --write .`\n- `npm test` / `npm run test:watch` — run Vitest unit tests / watch mode\n- `make deploy` / `make audit` — Ansible deploy (external `deployment/` repo) / `npm audit` + `npm outdated`\n\n## Hard rules\n- NEVER modify files outside /workspace\n- NEVER install packages without asking (dependencies are pinned, `engine-strict=true`)\n- NEVER commit secrets: auth tokens, API keys, or `.env*` files (gitignored); never log or print token or user data\n- NEVER hand-edit generated output — `build/` and `.svelte-kit/` are regenerated by `npm run build` / `npm run check`\n- When adding or changing UI strings, keep all four i18n files (`en`, `de`, `es`, `fr`) in sync — `npm run check` enforces it\n- When scope is unclear: ask, don't guess\n- Do not refactor things unrelated to the current task\n- Do not add abstractions not already present in the codebase\n\n</system-reminder>","metadata":{"preview":"# Assessment: time tracking\n\nid: whom\nstatus: open\ndate: 2026-10-10\nauthor: Leander Muskalla (brief) / implementation assessment\n\n## Scope\n\nThis is an **assessment-only** document. No source files were changed.\n\nThe brief: *\"the Time Tracking functionality is the weakest out of all of solyto's\nfeatures … visually confusing and inconsistent … the data model doesn't feel\nintuitive (the way you add projects, categories, entries) … the way you navigate\ndoesn't feel intuitive. It has a few good components, but it doesn't feel as easy\nto use and natural as the other features.\"*\n\nEvery claim below is grounded in the source with `file:line` references. This\nrepo is frontend-only, so `src/lib/types/time_tracking.ts` and\n`src/lib/config/apiRoutes.ts` are treated as the API contract. There are no","truncated":false,"loaded":["/workspace/docs/AGENTS.md"],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/workspace/docs/jobs/whom_assessment-time-tracking/assessment.md","text":"# Assessment: time tracking\n\nid: whom\nstatus: open\ndate: 2026-10-10\nauthor: Leander Muskalla (brief) / implementation assessment\n\n## Scope\n\nThis is an **assessment-only** document. No source files were changed.\n\nThe brief: *\"the Time Tracking functionality is the weakest out of all of solyto's\nfeatures … visually confusing and inconsistent … the data model doesn't feel\nintuitive (the way you add projects, categories, entries) … the way you navigate\ndoesn't feel intuitive. It has a few good components, but it doesn't feel as easy\nto use and natural as the other features.\"*\n\nEvery claim below is grounded in the source with `file:line` references. This\nrepo is frontend-only, so `src/lib/types/time_tracking.ts` and\n`src/lib/config/apiRoutes.ts` are treated as the API contract. There are no\nscreenshots or render reports for this job, so visual observations are derived\nfrom the templates/classes, not from rendered pixels.\n\nFiles reviewed: the whole of `src/lib/components/time-tracking/`, the three\n`src/routes/time-tracking/**` routes, `src/lib/state/TimeTracking.svelte.ts`,\n`src/lib/types/time_tracking.ts`, `src/lib/config/apiRoutes.ts`, the\n`src/lib/components/dashboard/widgets/TimeTrackingWidget.svelte` dashboard\nwidget, and the four i18n tables.\n\n---\n\n## Executive summary\n\n### What works well\n\n- **Clean store/contract layer.** `TimeTracking` keeps categories, projects,\n  entries, the active timer and statistics in one runes store, loads them in\n  parallel, and every mutation reloads the affected collections\n  (`src/lib/state/TimeTracking.svelte.ts:34-152`). The REST surface is small and\n  legible (`src/lib/config/apiRoutes.ts:271-294`).\n- **Projects → categories → entries is a genuinely useful shape.** Projects own\n  a many-to-many set of globally-defined categories, which is a reasonable way\n  to slice time by both a concrete bucket and a cross-cutting tag.\n- **The running-timer experience is honest and correct.** A single active timer\n  is derived from `entries.find(e => e.stopped_at === null)`\n  (`src/lib/state/TimeTracking.svelte.ts:57`); `ActiveTimer` recomputes elapsed\n  time from `started_at` every second (`ActiveTimer.svelte:15-28`) rather than\n  drifting with a counter, and cleans up its interval (`ActiveTimer.svelte:31-33`).\n- **The project-detail page is the best-organised surface.** Header with title,\n  description and category chips, a clear total-time card, date-grouped entries,\n  and a confirmed delete (`src/routes/time-tracking/[id]/+page.svelte:102-187`).\n- **Reuse of shared UI where it happens.** `ProjectCreate`/`EntryCreate`/\n  `EntryEdit` all use `ContentModal`, `TextInput`, `Select`, `DateTimeInput`,\n  and the standard button components; the feature is not a bespoke island.\n\n### What is broken or weak\n\n- **View state is invisible to the URL.** The Dashboard/Entries tabs live in\n  `activeTab` store state; both nav links point at the *same* href and only\n  mutate that state (`TimeTrackingNavigation.svelte:156-181`,\n  `+page.svelte:17-30`). Deep links, reloads, and back/forward all reset the view\n  to Dashboard. This is the single biggest structural problem and is unlike the\n  rest of the app (notes/todos/todos-mobile use URL-driven or open-state UI).\n- **Category management is hidden inside the navigation sidebar** and its two\n  key actions are non-discoverable: rename requires a **double-click**\n  (`TimeTrackingNavigation.svelte:300`) and colour editing is an inline third-party\n  picker (`:278-288`).\n- **The data model has dead and misleading fields.** `project.total_duration`\n  exists in the contract but is never read; durations are recomputed by hand in\n  three places. `has_exact_times` cannot be updated via the edit request at all.\n  There is an `uncategorized` fallback in two breakdown widgets for a state the\n  create API forbids (entries must have a `project_id`).\n- **Create vs. edit are duplicated and drift.** `EntryCreate` and `EntryEdit`\n  are ~90% the same file, and they disagree on timestamp handling (raw\n  `datetime-local` vs. `toISOString()`) and on whether `has_exact_times` is sent.\n- **The dashboard answers few real questions.** It stacks a timer, an unrelated\n  client-only Pomodoro, two per-project breakdowns, recent entries, and the full\n  statistics panel onto one page (`DashboardView.svelte:68-97`). The week\n  breakdown logic is duplicated verbatim in the dashboard-shell widget\n  (`dashboard/widgets/TimeTrackingWidget.svelte:24-52`).\n- **Consistency and accessibility gaps.** Hard-coded hex fallbacks (`#1dbda5`,\n  `#888`, `#9ca3af`), raw hand-styled `<input>`s next to shared form controls,\n  hover-only project controls, colour-contrast risk on category chips, icon-only\n  buttons without labels, and six unused i18n keys.\n\nThe short version: the **domain model is sound but under-used**, the **CRUD layer\nis duplicated and inconsistent**, the **navigation is stateful where it should be\nURL-driven**, and the **dashboard mixes concerns instead of answering questions**.\nNone of this needs a rewrite; it needs a coherent pass to make the model the\nsingle source of truth, unify create/edit, move view state into the URL, and\nsplit the dashboard's concerns.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Navigation & information architecture\n\n### 1.1 Structure\n\n- `/time-tracking` → `+layout.svelte` mounts the navigation sidebar and the\n  project-create modal, and loads the store (`+layout.svelte:10,22-26`).\n- `+page.svelte` renders either `DashboardView` or `GroupedEntries` depending on\n  `tt.activeTab` (`+page.svelte:17-30`).\n- `/time-tracking/[id]` renders the project detail (`[id]/+page.svelte:30-32`),\n  with an effect that redirects to the list if the project disappears\n  (`[id]/+page.svelte:52-56`).\n- The sidebar (`TimeTrackingNavigation.svelte`) holds Dashboard/Entries links,\n  the project list, and the category manager.\n\nThis is a standard \"sidebar + content\" IA and is easy to understand. The problems\nare in how it is *driven*.\n\n### 1.2 View state is not in the URL (high impact)\n\nBoth navigation entries resolve to the **same** route:\n\n```svelte\n<!-- TimeTrackingNavigation.svelte:156-168 (Dashboard) and :169-181 (Entries) -->\n<a href={resolve(urls.timeTracking)} onclick={() => { tt.activeTab = 'dashboard'; handleNavigation(); }}>\n<a href={resolve(urls.timeTracking)} onclick={() => { tt.activeTab = 'entries';  handleNavigation(); }}>\n```\n\n`activeTab` defaults to `'dashboard'` (`TimeTracking.svelte.ts:26`) and is never\npersisted or derived from `page.url`. Consequences:\n\n- **Reload** always lands on Dashboard, even if the user was on Entries.\n- **Deep-linking** to the entries view is impossible.\n- **Back/forward** cannot restore the view: both links push the *same* URL, and\n  the store is not reset, so history navigation silently does nothing (or leaves\n  a stale tab).\n- The active-tab highlight depends on `activeTab`, so it too can desync from the\n  URL (`TimeTrackingNavigation.svelte:159-160,172-173`).\n\nSibling features do better: `/notes` and `/todos` represent the selected\ncategory/view through routes (`src/routes/notes/[id]`) or through explicit\nopen/stack state with real overlays, and use the URL to restore context. Time\ntracking is the outlier.\n\n**Suggested direction (not code):** make the tab part of the route (e.g. a nested\nroute or a `?tab=` search param read via `page.url.searchParams`), or remove the\ndual-tab model entirely and treat Entries as a route. At minimum, derive\n`activeTab` from the URL and write it on click.\n\n### 1.3 Mobile drawer uses a different mechanism than every other feature\n\n`TimeTrackingNavigation` toggles the drawer by mutating inline style:\n\n```svelte\n<!-- TimeTrackingNavigation.svelte:68-78 -->\nfunction toggleMobile() { if (navigation) navigation.style.display = 'block'; }\nfunction handleNavigation() { if (navigation && navigation.style.display === 'block') navigation.style.display = 'none'; }\n```\n\nThe container is `hidden … lg:flex` (`:142-146`), so on mobile it is revealed by\nthe inline `display:block`; it is closed by re-setting `display='none'`.\n\nEvery other feature uses an `open` `$state` flag plus `{#if open}` and a Svelte\n`transition:fly`/`fade`, e.g. `TodoNavigationMobile.svelte:23,63-69` and\n`NoteNavigationMobile.svelte:18,42-46`. The time-tracking drawer:\n\n- has no enter/exit transition (the others slide in),\n- can get stuck in the wrong display mode across a resize (inline `display`\n  outranks the `lg:flex` class),\n- exposes nothing to the DOM/state model that tests or focus management can use.\n\nThis is a concrete instance of the brief's \"doesn't feel intuitive/consistent\".\n\n### 1.4 Affordances hidden in the sidebar\n\nProject create/edit/delete and *all* category management live inside the\nnavigation drawer (`TimeTrackingNavigation.svelte:184-314`). On mobile the drawer\nis off-screen until the hamburger is tapped; on desktop it is a 64-unit column.\nConsequences:\n\n- Create-project is a bare `+` icon next to the \"Projects\" heading\n  (`:184-198`), with no label.\n- Project edit/delete are **hover-only** (`opacity-0 … group-hover:opacity-100`,\n  `:215-217`) — unreachable by touch until the row is tapped, and effectively\n  invisible to new users.\n- Category rename is a **double-click** on the label (`:298-303`); category\n  delete is an always-visible trash icon that fires immediately (`:306-311`),\n  so the destructive action is *more* discoverable than the safe one.\n- The category colour picker is embedded in the list row (`:278-288`) and\n  commits only on click outside (`:127-132`); leaving the page without clicking\n  outside discards the choice (no `onDestroy` flush).\n\n### 1.5 Where each action is reachable (matrix)\n\n| Action | Dashboard tab | Entries tab | Project detail | Nav sidebar |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Start/stop timer | `TimerWidget` / `ActiveTimer` | — | — | — |\n| Add entry | — | `+ Add Entry` (`+page.svelte:23-26`) | `Add Entry` (`[id]/+page.svelte:139-142`) | — |\n| Edit entry | — (recent entries are read-only, `DashboardView.svelte:91-93`) | via `Entry` edit (`GroupedEntries.svelte:50-56`) | — (delete only, `[id]/+page.svelte:179`) | — |\n| Delete entry | — | `Entry` trash (`Entry.svelte:64-66`) | `Entry` trash (`[id]/+page.svelte:179`) | — |\n| Create project | timer/breakdown empty states only | same | — | `+` button (`:189-197`) |\n| Edit project | — | — | pen button (`[id]/+page.svelte:143-147`) | hover pen (`:218-228`) |\n| Delete project | — | — | confirmed (`[id]/+page.svelte:148-152,90-100`) | immediate (`:229-238`) |\n| Manage categories | — | — | — | add/rename/recolour/delete (`:244-314`) |\n\nThe matrix shows the inconsistency directly: the *same* project can be deleted\nwith confirmation on one screen and without on another, and entries are editable\nfrom exactly one of three surfaces that show them.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Data model & domain semantics\n\n### 2.1 The model (the contract)\n\n```ts\n// src/lib/types/time_tracking.ts\ninterface TimeTrackingCategory { id: number; title: string; color: string | null; … }\ninterface TimeTrackingProject  { id: string; title; description; categories: TimeTrackingCategory[];\n                                 total_duration: number; … }            // :9-17\ninterface TimeTrackingEntry    { id: string; description; started_at; stopped_at: string | null;\n                                 duration_minutes: number; has_exact_times: boolean;\n                                 project: TimeTrackingProject | null; … } // :19-29\n```\n\n- Categories are **global** (fetched once from `/time-tracking/categories`), and\n  a project links to them via `category_ids` on create/update\n  (`CreateTimeTrackingProjectRequest.category_ids?: number[]`, `:44`).\n- An entry **must** belong to a project: `CreateTimeTrackingEntryRequest` makes\n  `project_id: string` required (`:53-60`), and the timer's `StartTimerRequest`\n  too (`:70-73`). Yet the read model still types `project` as nullable (`:26`).\n\nThis is a reasonable model. The issues are in how the client *uses* it.\n\n### 2.2 `total_duration` is dead weight\n\n`TimeTrackingProject.total_duration` (`:14`) is declared but **never read**\nanywhere in `src/`. Instead the client recomputes totals by hand in at least\nthree places:\n\n- `TimeTrackingNavigation.svelte:38-47` builds a `projectDurations` map by\n  filtering and reducing entries.\n- `src/routes/time-tracking/[id]/+page.svelte:44-50` reduces the same entries for\n  the project's total.\n- `DashboardView.svelte` and `TimeTrackingWidget.svelte` re-derive week/today\n  totals from raw entries (`DashboardView.svelte:43-65`,\n  `TimeTrackingWidget.svelte:41-52`).\n\nSo there are **two competing sources of truth** for the same number (API field\nvs. client reduction), and the client chose the more expensive one. The API also\nexposes a statistics endpoint (`statistics: '/time-tracking/entries/statistics'`,\n`apiRoutes.ts:292`) that the dashboard ignores for its own breakdowns.\n\n**Suggested direction:** pick one authority. Either consume `total_duration` and\nthe statistics endpoint, or formally deprecate the field. Do not keep both.\n\n### 2.3 The `uncategorized` state cannot exist\n\nTwo breakdown widgets carry a fallback for entries with no project:\n\n```ts\nconst key   = project?.title ?? ts.get.timeTracking.uncategorized;\nconst color = project?.categories?.[0]?.color ?? '#9ca3af';\n```\n\n`DashboardView.svelte:32-33`, `dashboard/widgets/TimeTrackingWidget.svelte:31`.\nBut the create contract requires `project_id`, and the timer requires a project,\nso a project-less entry should be impossible. Either the fallback is dead code\n(remove it and simplify) or there is a real server-returned case the client\nshould handle deliberately (a proper \"unassigned\" bucket with UX). Right now it\nis quietly carrying a hard-coded grey.\n\n### 2.4 `has_exact_times` cannot be edited\n\nThe flag is:\n- **sent** on create (`EntryCreate.svelte:63,89`, `CreateTimeTrackingEntryRequest.has_exact_times`),\n- **read** for display (`Entry.svelte:49-54`),\n- but **absent** from `UpdateTimeTrackingEntryRequest` (`types/time_tracking.ts:62-68`).\n\n`EntryEdit` therefore never sends it (`EntryEdit.svelte:68-77`). Editing an\nentry to add exact start/stop times updates `started_at`/`stopped_at` but leaves\n`has_exact_times` at its old value, so the UI may keep showing (or hiding) the\ntime range incorrectly after an edit. The domain concept (\"do we have real clock\ntimes or just a duration?\") leaks into the UI without a way to keep it correct.\n\n### 2.5 Backdating is misleading (`buildTimestamps`)\n\n```ts\n// EntryCreate.svelte:65-76\nconst now  = new Date();\nconst end  = new Date(`${date}T${now.getHours()}:${now.getMinutes()}:00`);\nconst start = new Date(end.getTime() - durationMinutes * 60000);\nreturn { started_at: start.toISOString(), stopped_at: end.toISOString() };\n```\n\nThe **calendar day** comes from the user's `date`, but the **time of day** is\nalways *the moment the entry is created*. So:\n\n- A duration-only entry added for a past day is stamped with today's wall-clock\n  time on that past day — an arbitrary-looking start/stop that the user never\n  chose and cannot see (the entry renders as a bare duration unless\n  `has_exact_times` is set, `Entry.svelte:49-54`).\n- If the duration exceeds the current time-of-day, `start` rolls into the\n  **previous calendar day**, so an entry \"for 2026-10-01\" can silently begin on\n  2026-09-30. The date grouping (`[id]/+page.svelte:58-70`,\n  `GroupedEntries.svelte:18-30`) then places it under the wrong day.\n- The visible `date` field stays on screen even when the exact-times section is\n  expanded (`EntryCreate.svelte:130-139` vs. `:149-164`), where it is ignored —\n  the user can change it with no effect.\n\n**Suggested direction:** for duration-only entries, anchor at a defined\ntime (e.g. local midnight, or noon, or the user's chosen time) and compute the\nday from that anchor, not from \"now\". Hide the date field when exact times are in\nuse, or make the two inputs one coherent control.\n\n### 2.6 Category colour is overloaded as the project colour\n\nA project has **many** categories, each with a colour, but the breakdowns colour\nthe project by its **first** category:\n\n```ts\nconst color = project?.categories?.[0]?.color ?? '#9ca3af';\n// DashboardView.svelte:33, TimeTrackingWidget.svelte:31\n```\n\nIf a project's first category changes, the project's chart colour changes. If a\nproject has several categories, the other colours vanish. The statistics endpoint\ndoes return a per-project `color` (`TimeTrackingStatisticsEntry.color`,\n`types/time_tracking.ts:75-80`) which `StatisticsView:47` uses — so the donut and\nthe breakdowns can disagree about a project's colour. A project needs its own\nidentity/colour, separate from its category tags.\n\n### 2.7 What the model makes easy vs. awkward\n\n**Easy / natural:** start a timer against a project in one click; add a manual\nduration; tag projects with reusable categories; view date-grouped history.\n\n**Awkward:** there is no way to record \"I worked on X\" without first creating a\nproject; categories are mandatory to get a coloured chart (else grey); the\nproject's own total is recomputed rather than trusted; and \"when did this happen\"\ncannot be expressed honestly without discovering the hidden \"started at / stopped\nat\" disclosure.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Create / edit / manage flows (CRUD friction)\n\n### 3.1 `ProjectCreate` doubles as edit (acceptable, with rough edges)\n\nOne modal handles create and edit via `projectId`\n(`ProjectCreate.svelte:13-18`), titles itself from `existingProject`\n(`:69`), and pre-fills fields. Good.\n\nRough edges:\n- Empty title silently no-ops: `if (!title.trim()) return;` (`:44-45`) — no\n  message, the Save button appears to do nothing.\n- Category selection is a `+` that opens a dropdown of remaining categories\n  (`:106-133`); removing a category is an unlabelled `×` (`:98-103`). Both use\n  the generic dashed-border style rather than a shared control.\n- Colours are hard-coded in two places: chips fall back to `#888` (`:95`) and\n  the dropdown dots render only `{#if cat.color}` (`:121-126`), so a category\n  without a colour shows no dot at all.\n\n### 3.2 `EntryCreate` vs. `EntryEdit`: ~90% duplicate, and they disagree\n\nThe two files share the same structure: project `Select`, description\n`TextInput`, duration amount + unit, an exact-times disclosure with two\n`DateTimeInput`s, and a save button. But:\n\n| Aspect | `EntryCreate` | `EntryEdit` |\n|---|---|---|\n| Date field | present (`:130-139`) | absent |\n| Default project | first project (`:33`) | entry's project, else first (`:31`) |\n| Exact-times initial state | collapsed (`:30`) | collapsed **even if `entry.has_exact_times`** (`:32`) |\n| Sends `has_exact_times` | yes (`:89`) | no (field absent from update type) |\n| Exact timestamps sent as | raw `datetime-local` value (`:67`) | `new Date(...).toISOString()` (`:75-76`) |\n| `started_at` init | empty | `entry.started_at.slice(0,16)` (`:33`) |\n\nTwo concrete bugs fall out of this:\n\n1. **Timezone handling differs.** `EntryCreate` sends the raw `datetime-local`\n   string (`\"2026-10-01T09:00\"`, no zone), while `EntryEdit` converts via\n   `toISOString()` (UTC). The same times entered in create and edit produce\n   different instants. Worse, `EntryEdit` initialises the control from\n   `entry.started_at.slice(0,16)` — an ISO/UTC string — and feeds it to a\n   `datetime-local` input, which interprets it as *local* time, so an existing\n   entry's times shift by the UTC offset the moment the disclosure is opened.\n2. **Editing the date is impossible** without expanding the exact-times\n   disclosure (there is no date field), even though the entry has a date.\n\n`EntryCreate` also `$derived`s `hasExactTimes = showExactTimes && !!(startedAt && stoppedAt)`\n(`:63`) but only sends it on create; and the disclosure can be open with only one\ntimestamp filled, in which case `hasExactTimes` is false and `buildTimestamps`\nfalls back to \"now\" (`:65-76`) while the half-filled inputs remain on screen.\n\n### 3.3 Silent validation\n\nBoth entry forms share the same silent guard:\n\n```ts\nif (!projectId || durationMinutes <= 0) return;   // EntryCreate.svelte:79; EntryEdit.svelte:64\n```\n\nNo error, no disabled-button feedback. A user who types a duration but hasn't\npicked a project (or types 0) clicks Save and nothing happens. `TextButton` is\nalways enabled (`EntryCreate.svelte:166`). This is a recurring source of\n\"the app feels unresponsive\".\n\n### 3.4 Default-to-first-project\n\nThree components silently default the project to `tt.projects[0]`:\n\n- `TimerWidget.svelte:14`\n- `EntryCreate.svelte:33`\n- `EntryEdit.svelte:31` (fallback only)\n\nThe project order is `tt.projects` as returned by the API; there is no \"most\nrecent\" or alphabetic guarantee. So a manual entry or a timer can be silently\nbooked against whichever project happens to be first — a data-integrity risk\nthat is easy to miss because the chosen project is shown but not obvious.\n\n### 3.5 Delete inconsistency\n\n| Thing | Confirmation | Location |\n|---|---|---|\n| Project (detail page) | `ConfirmationModal` (`[id]/+page.svelte:90-100`) | explicit |\n| Project (sidebar) | **none** (`TimeTrackingNavigation.svelte:229-238`) | immediate |\n| Category | **none** (`:306-311`) | immediate |\n| Entry | **none** (`Entry.svelte:64-66`) | immediate |\n\nThe project case is the starkest: deleting from the detail page asks and removes\nassociated entries (`deletion_description`, `i18n/en.ts:1109-1110`), but the same\ndestructive call from the sidebar is one click with no prompt and no undo. Entry\nand category deletes have no undo anywhere.\n\n### 3.6 Editing availability\n\n- Entries are editable **only** from the Entries tab, via `GroupedEntries` →\n  `Entry showEdit` (`GroupedEntries.svelte:50-56`).\n- The dashboard's \"Recent Entries\" are read-only (`DashboardView.svelte:91-93`).\n- The project detail page shows entries with delete but **not** edit\n  (`[id]/+page.svelte:179`, `Entry showDelete` only).\n\nSo a user who spots a mistake while looking at a project's history must navigate\nto the Entries tab to fix it — the surface that shows the entry doesn't let you\nedit it.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Timer, dashboard & statistics\n\n### 4.1 Timer\n\n- `TimerWidget` shows `ActiveTimer` when `tt.activeTimer` is set, otherwise a\n  project `Select`, a description input, and a Start button\n  (`TimerWidget.svelte:34-56`). The mutual exclusivity is correct and readable.\n- `ActiveTimer` derives elapsed from `started_at` each tick\n  (`ActiveTimer.svelte:15-28`) and destroys its interval (`:31-33`) — sound.\n- The start form resets `timerDescription` after a start (`TimerWidget.svelte:29`),\n  but does **not** reset/re-select the project, which is fine for repeat starts.\n\nHonest state: yes — one timer, derived from entries, not a separate flag. The one\ngap is that `startTimer` (`TimeTracking.svelte.ts:132-136`) has no client guard\nagainst starting a second timer; it relies on the UI not offering Start when a\ntimer is active. Acceptable, but a defensive check would be cheap.\n\n### 4.2 Pomodoro is a fourth, unintegrated timer\n\n`PomodoroWidget` is a fully client-side interval timer (25/5/15 min, beep,\nsession dots: `PomodoroWidget.svelte:13-16,61-76,100-121`). It:\n\n- creates **no** time entry and links to **no** project (`:1-126` — it only\n  imports `getTranslation`),\n- is not persisted (reload loses it; `onDestroy` just clears the interval,\n  `:123-125`),\n- duplicates the concept of \"a timer\" beside the real one, with a *different*\n  visual language (teal/amber, own buttons) than `ActiveTimer` (primary-coloured).\n\nFor a feature whose purpose is \"track time spent on projects\", a prominent timer\nthat records nothing is actively confusing — it competes with the real timer in\nthe same grid (`DashboardView.svelte:69-75`). Either wire it to create/link an\nentry (and share the timer UI), or demote it out of the time-tracking dashboard.\n\n### 4.3 Dashboard composition mixes concerns\n\n`DashboardView` is a single column that stacks (`DashboardView.svelte:68-97`):\n\n1. a 4-up grid of Timer, Pomodoro, Today-breakdown, This-Week-breakdown,\n2. recent entries (read-only),\n3. the full `StatisticsView`.\n\nSo the \"Dashboard\" contains a full analytics panel, and the standalone stats\nsurface doesn't exist as its own tab. `StatisticsView` also fires its own API\ncall on mount (`StatisticsView.svelte:70-72`), so merely opening the dashboard\ntriggers a statistics request.\n\nThe layout also has a class bug: the breakdown widgets declare\n`lg:col-span-1` (`TimeBreakdownWidget.svelte:18`) in a grid that is\n`md:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-4` (`DashboardView.svelte:69`); the Timer and\nPomodoro wrappers also say `lg:col-span-1` (`:70,73`). Everything claims one\ncolumn, which happens to work for exactly four items but is fragile if the set\nchanges.\n\n### 4.4 Breakdowns duplicate logic and colour arbitrarily\n\n`DashboardView.groupByProject` (`:26-41`) and\n`TimeTrackingWidget.groupByProject` (`:24-39`) are the same function, including\nthe `#9ca3af` fallback and the first-category colour rule. `TimeBreakdownWidget`\njust renders whatever list it is given (`TimeBreakdownWidget.svelte:29-51`).\nGrouping happens by **project title**, so two projects with the same title merge.\n\n### 4.5 Statistics\n\n- Embedded, not a tab (see 4.3).\n- Date range uses two raw `<input type=\"date\">`s with bespoke classes\n  (`StatisticsView.svelte:89-103`) rather than a shared date control; defaults are\n  computed from `toISOString()` (`:15-19`), i.e. UTC, so users east/west of UTC\n  can get a one-day-off default range.\n- The **category filter filters *projects*** by whether any of their categories\n  match (`:30-37`) and then recomputes both the donut and the category bar chart\n  from the filtered set (`:43-63`). Its label says \"Filter by Category\" but the\n  effect is \"restrict to projects tagged with these categories\" — defensible, but\n  it silently drops projects that have the category plus others' time.\n- The `DoughnutChart` uses the server's per-project `color` (`:47`) while the\n  breakdown widgets use first-category colour (see 2.6), so colours are not\n  consistent across the page.\n- Category bar values are converted to hours with one decimal\n  (`:67`, `Math.round((c.minutes / 60) * 10) / 10`) while everything else is\n  `h m` text — a units inconsistency inside one view.\n- `loadStatistics` interpolates `from`/`to` directly into a query string\n  (`TimeTracking.svelte.ts:147-152`) without encoding; harmless for date-only\n  values but worth noting.\n\n### 4.6 Dashboard shell widget\n\n`dashboard/widgets/TimeTrackingWidget.svelte` is a *separate* week-breakdown\nwidget used on the home dashboard (`QuickGlance.svelte:14`). It re-implements the\nsame grouping, colour fallback and totals as `DashboardView` (`:24-52`) and calls\n`tt.load()` itself (`:54-59`), so the store can be loaded twice. This is a\ntextbook DRY violation and a maintenance hazard: fixing a rule in one place\nmisses the other.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Visual & interaction consistency, accessibility, i18n\n\n### 5.1 Shared controls vs. hand-rolled inputs\n\nThe feature is inconsistent about using its own design system:\n\n| Control | Uses shared component? | Evidence |\n|---|---|---|\n| Project title/description | ✅ `TextInput` | `ProjectCreate.svelte:79,85` |\n| Entry description | ✅ `TextInput` | `EntryCreate.svelte:112`, `EntryEdit.svelte:98` |\n| Project selector | ✅ `Select` | `EntryCreate.svelte:106`, `TimerWidget.svelte:42` |\n| Duration amount | ❌ raw `<input type=\"number\">` | `EntryCreate.svelte:119-126`, `EntryEdit.svelte:105-111` |\n| Date | ❌ raw `<input type=\"date\">` | `EntryCreate.svelte:134-138`, `StatisticsView.svelte:89-103` |\n| Timer description | ❌ raw `<input type=\"text\">` | `TimerWidget.svelte:43-48` |\n| Category inline rename | ❌ raw `<input type=\"text\">` | `TimeTrackingNavigation.svelte:290-296` |\n\nThe raw inputs copy the shared classes by hand, which is why they have already\ndrifted: `EntryCreate.svelte:125` uses `focus:ring-d-lightblue` and\n`dark:focus:ring-c-primary` — identical to `TextInput.svelte:30`, so the drift is\ncosmetic today, but nothing keeps them in sync. `TimerWidget`'s input uses the\nsame recipe (`:47`). There is no shared `NumberInput`/`DateInput`, so adding one\n(or reusing `TextInput` with a `type`) would remove the duplication.\n\n### 5.2 Hard-coded colours\n\nColour literals are scattered and fall back differently per component:\n\n- `#1dbda5` (brand teal) is the category fallback in the nav\n  (`TimeTrackingNavigation.svelte:61,84,130`) and in the statistics filter\n  (`StatisticsView.svelte:134,139`).\n- `#888` is the fallback in `ProjectCreate.svelte:95` and on the detail page\n  category chips (`[id]/+page.svelte:129`).\n- `#9ca3af` is the breakdown fallback (`DashboardView.svelte:33`,\n  `TimeTrackingWidget.svelte:31`).\n\nThree different \"unknown colour\" greys/teals for the same concept. The codebase\nalready exposes theming via CSS variables (`--color-c-primary`, etc.), so these\nshould be variables, not literals.\n\n### 5.3 Hover-only and touch-hostile affordances\n\n- Project edit/delete hidden until hover (`TimeTrackingNavigation.svelte:216`).\n  On touch there is no hover, so these only appear after a tap that also\n  navigates. This is the clearest \"not natural on mobile\" finding.\n- Category rename requires double-click (`:300`) — not discoverable, not\n  available via keyboard, and awkward on touch (double-tap also zooms).\n- `InlineEditButton`/`InlineDeleteButton` reveal their actual clickable button\n  only on `group-hover` (`InlineEditButton.svelte:13`,\n  `InlineDeleteButton.svelte:12-13`); they render an inert icon underneath, so\n  the visible icon is not the button. That is both an a11y and a hit-target\n  problem where they're used (entry rows, project header).\n\n### 5.4 Accessibility\n\n- **Double-click to rename a category** (`TimeTrackingNavigation.svelte:300`) is\n  a keyboard and screen-reader dead end; nothing signals the action.\n- **Icon-only buttons without accessible names**: project add (`:189-197`),\n  project edit/delete (`:218-238`), category add (`:249-258`), category delete\n  (`:306-311`), and the `Inline*` buttons carry only SVG icons — no `aria-label`\n  or visible text.\n- **Colour contrast**: category chips render `text-xs text-white` on whatever\n  `cat.color` is (`[id]/+page.svelte:127-131`, `ProjectCreate.svelte:93-96`).\n  A light category colour gives white-on-light text with no contrast guard; the\n  statistics filter pills have the same issue (`StatisticsView.svelte:129-142`).\n- **Form labelling**: the entry/project fields use plain `<span>` labels, not\n  `<label for>`; none of the inputs are programmatically associated\n  (`EntryCreate.svelte:102-139`, `ProjectCreate.svelte:75-86`).\n- The mobile drawer's `display` toggling (1.3) gives no focus trap/restore, so\n  keyboard focus can move behind the open drawer.\n\n### 5.5 i18n\n\nAll four locales define the same key set and are in sync (verified for\n`timeTracking`: identical keys across `en.ts`, `de.ts`, `es.ts`, `fr.ts`) — good.\n\nHowever six `timeTracking` keys are **defined but never referenced** anywhere in\n`src/` (excluding the i18n tables):\n\n| Key | `en.ts` line | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| `add_category` | 1078 | UI uses an icon-only `+` instead |\n| `edit_category` | 1079 | rename is double-click, no label |\n| `color` | 1082 | colour picker is unlabelled |\n| `duration_minutes` | 1086 | duration uses a number + unit select |\n| `category` | 1088 | singular; only `categories` is used |\n| `this_month` | 1107 | dashboard shows Today/This Week only |\n\nAdditionally, `dev.features.timeTracking` tips (`timer_tip`, `projects_tip`,\n`pomodoro_tip`, `en.ts:1062-1064`) are not consumed by any component: the\nwelcome tour reads tips from `welcome_tour.steps`, not `dev.features`\n(`WelcomeTour.svelte:73-78`), and `OnboardingModalStepFeatures` uses\n`welcome_tour` keys (`OnboardingModalStepFeatures.svelte:22-35`). So the\nPomodoro tip in particular documents a feature users are never told about — and\nthe whole `dev.features.timeTracking` block is orphaned. These are dead strings\nthat still cost translation effort; the unused ones should be removed (or wired\nup).\n\n### 5.6 Heading/spacing conventions\n\n`DashboardView` uses `Heading` for \"Recent Entries\" (`:90`) but the statistics\nheading is inside `StatisticsView` (`:83`); section titles elsewhere are bare\n`<span>`s (`TimeBreakdownWidget.svelte:19`, `Projects`/`Categories` in the nav\n`:186,246`). Heading levels are therefore inconsistent across one page. Spacing\nis also ad-hoc (`gap-8` outer, `gap-4` grid, `gap-6`/`gap-3` sections mixed), so\nthe vertical rhythm varies between the dashboard and the project detail page.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Prioritised follow-up recommendations\n\nRanked by impact/effort. Each is a candidate for a separate implementation job;\nnone is in scope here.\n\n| # | Recommendation | Impact | Effort | Files |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **Make view state URL-driven.** Move Dashboard/Entries (and ideally the project selection) into the route or a `?tab=` param; derive `activeTab` from `page.url` so reload/deep-link/back work. | High | M | `TimeTrackingNavigation.svelte`, `+page.svelte`, `TimeTracking.svelte.ts`, `urls.ts` |\n| 2 | **Unify `EntryCreate`/`EntryEdit` into one component** that takes an optional `entry`; fix the timestamp/timezone mismatch (always send ISO) and add a real date control. | High | M | `EntryCreate.svelte`, `EntryEdit.svelte`, `GroupedEntries.svelte`, `+page.svelte`, `[id]/+page.svelte` |\n| 3 | **Fix backdating semantics** in `buildTimestamps` (defined anchor, correct day, hide the date field when exact times are used). | High | S | `EntryCreate.svelte` |\n| 4 | **Single source of truth for durations/colours**: consume `project.total_duration`/statistics, give projects their own colour, delete the duplicated `groupByProject` and the `#9ca3af`/`#888`/`#1dbda5` literals. | High | M | `TimeTracking.svelte.ts`, `DashboardView.svelte`, `TimeTrackingWidget.svelte`, `TimeBreakdownWidget.svelte`, `StatisticsView.svelte`, `types/time_tracking.ts` |\n| 5 | **Give Pomodoro a purpose or a home**: either link it to an entry/timer with shared UI, or move it out of the time-tracking dashboard. | Medium | M | `PomodoroWidget.svelte`, `DashboardView.svelte` |\n| 6 | **Confirm-or-undo for destructive actions.** Route all project deletes through `ConfirmationModal`; add undo for entry/category deletes. | Medium | S | `TimeTrackingNavigation.svelte`, `Entry.svelte` |\n| 7 | **Surface entry editing where entries are shown** (dashboard recent entries, project detail). | Medium | S | `DashboardView.svelte`, `[id]/+page.svelte`, `Entry.svelte` |\n| 8 | **Replace the sidebar's `display` toggle with the app's `open`-state + transition drawer pattern** used by todos/notes. | Medium | S | `TimeTrackingNavigation.svelte` |\n| 9 | **Make category management discoverable and accessible**: replace double-click-to-rename with an explicit affordance, add `aria-label`s to icon buttons, guard category-chip contrast, avoid hover-only project controls. | Medium | M | `TimeTrackingNavigation.svelte`, `ProjectCreate.svelte`, `Entry.svelte`, `[id]/+page.svelte`, `InlineEdit/DeleteButton.svelte` |\n| 10 | **Add explicit form validation feedback** instead of silent `return` on invalid save (disable Save or show a message). | Medium | S | `EntryCreate.svelte`, `EntryEdit.svelte`, `ProjectCreate.svelte` |\n| 11 | **Reuse shared form controls** for number/date/timer inputs (or add a `NumberInput`/`DateInput`), removing hand-copied classes. | Low | S | `EntryCreate.svelte`, `EntryEdit.svelte`, `TimerWidget.svelte`, `StatisticsView.svelte` |\n| 12 | **Handle or delete the `uncategorized` path** and the un-editable `has_exact_times` flag so the UI matches the contract. | Low | S | `DashboardView.svelte`, `TimeTrackingWidget.svelte`, `types/time_tracking.ts`, `EntryEdit.svelte` |\n| 13 | **Remove (or wire up) dead i18n keys** `add_category`, `edit_category`, `color`, `duration_minutes`, `category`, `this_month`, and the orphaned `dev.features.timeTracking` tips, across all four locales. | Low | S | `i18n/{en,de,es,fr}.ts` |\n| 14 | **Fix UTC-based default dates** (`new Date().toISOString()`) in entry and statistics defaults to local time. | Low | S | `EntryCreate.svelte`, `StatisticsView.svelte` |\n| 15 | **Normalise heading/spacing** in the dashboard and detail page via the shared `Heading` component. | Low | S | `DashboardView.svelte`, `StatisticsView.svelte`, `TimeBreakdownWidget.svelte` |\n\nSuggested sequencing: 1–4 together form the \"make the foundations coherent\" pass\nand unlock most of the rest; 5–10 are the \"natural-feeling interactions\" pass;\n11–15 are polish.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Conclusion\n\nTime Tracking is not a bad feature with broken plumbing — it is a good domain\nmodel wrapped in a UI that hides its model, duplicates it, and drives view state\noutside the URL. The clearest wins are structural and cheap to state: put the\nview in the URL, unify create/edit, trust the API's own totals and colours, make\ncategory management visible, and stop the Pomodoro timer from competing with the\nreal one. The store/contract layer and the running-timer widget are solid\nfoundations to build on.\n\n**Scope note:** per the brief, this is an assessment only — **no source files\nwere changed**. All recommendations are candidates for follow-up implementation\njobs.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":687,"totalLines":687,"truncated":false}},"title":"docs/jobs/whom_assessment-time-tracking/assessment.md","time":{"start":1791623566178,"end":1791623566292}},"id":"prt_1251613120011yIhem3JoEQxWM","sessionID":"ses_edaec2704ffeWB9idQ36MdrQEO","messageID":"msg_125160cce001v9mNLHw8lU7PN5"}}
